1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,612
This is a PDF invoice.

2
00:00:01,612 --> 00:00:06,877
And in about one second, Python just read every line of it, and pulled out the total.

3
00:00:06,877 --> 00:00:10,098
No copying, no retyping, no squinting at the screen.

4
00:00:10,098 --> 00:00:13,442
Now imagine doing that for a hundred invoices at once.

5
00:00:13,442 --> 00:00:15,362
That's what you'll build today.

6
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:26,716
In the next ten minutes, you'll learn to get the text out of any PDF, find one value inside it, pull a whole table straight into Excel, and then process a folder full of PDFs in one go.

7
00:00:26,716 --> 00:00:28,731
Step by step, no experience needed.

8
00:00:28,731 --> 00:00:33,799
And I'll show you the PDFs that refuse to give up their text, and what to do about them.

9
00:00:34,499 --> 00:00:35,458
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:35,458 --> 00:00:36,956
Install one free library.

11
00:00:36,956 --> 00:00:38,693
Read all the text from a PDF.

12
00:00:38,693 --> 00:00:40,371
Find the one value you need.

13
00:00:40,371 --> 00:00:43,128
Extract a table, and save it as an Excel file.

14
00:00:43,128 --> 00:00:44,206
Then, the big one.

15
00:00:44,206 --> 00:00:49,420
Loop through a whole folder of invoices, and build a summary spreadsheet automatically.

16
00:00:50,119 --> 00:00:51,697
Why not just copy and paste?

17
00:00:51,697 --> 00:00:54,400
Try it with a table, and the columns fall apart.

18
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,230
Everything lands in one cell, or the numbers jump to the wrong rows.

19
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:00,652
And with fifty PDFs, it takes an afternoon.

20
00:01:00,652 --> 00:01:08,368
Python reads the PDF the way the computer stores it, so the rows and columns come out clean, and fifty files take the same effort as one.

21
00:01:09,068 --> 00:01:10,701
You need Python installed.

22
00:01:10,701 --> 00:01:14,218
If you don't have it yet, it's free from python dot org.

23
00:01:14,218 --> 00:01:18,238
Open a terminal and type pip install pdfplumber pandas openpyxl.

24
00:01:18,238 --> 00:01:19,620
Pdfplumber reads PDFs.

25
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:21,127
Pandas organizes tables.

26
00:01:21,127 --> 00:01:23,828
And openpyxl lets pandas write Excel files.

27
00:01:23,828 --> 00:01:25,901
Then check it with this one line.

28
00:01:25,901 --> 00:01:28,853
If it prints two version numbers, you're ready.

29
00:01:29,553 --> 00:01:32,284
Here are the practice files, all in one folder.

30
00:01:32,284 --> 00:01:38,153
An invoice, a two page sales report, a scanned receipt, and a folder called invoices, with five more.

31
00:01:38,153 --> 00:01:40,245
Your scripts go in this same folder.

32
00:01:40,245 --> 00:01:44,836
That's important, because Python looks for files in the folder you run it from.

33
00:01:45,536 --> 00:01:46,045
Step one.

34
00:01:46,045 --> 00:01:47,346
Create step one dot py.

35
00:01:47,346 --> 00:01:48,364
Import pdfplumber.

36
00:01:48,364 --> 00:01:52,153
Then, with pdfplumber dot open and the file name, you open the PDF.

37
00:01:52,153 --> 00:01:55,434
The with keyword closes the file for you when you're done.

38
00:01:55,434 --> 00:01:57,526
pdf dot pages is a list of the pages.

39
00:01:57,526 --> 00:02:00,015
Print its length, to see how many there are.

40
00:02:00,015 --> 00:02:01,429
Then take the first page.

41
00:02:01,429 --> 00:02:05,275
Remember, Python counts from zero, so the first page is pages, zero.

42
00:02:05,275 --> 00:02:08,329
And extract text gives you all its text as one string.

43
00:02:09,029 --> 00:02:09,440
Run it.

44
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:10,086
Pages, one.

45
00:02:10,086 --> 00:02:12,729
And there's the whole invoice, as plain text.

46
00:02:12,729 --> 00:02:20,188
The shop name, the invoice number, the date, the customer, every item with its quantity and price, and the total at the bottom.

47
00:02:20,188 --> 00:02:22,008
Line by line, in reading order.

48
00:02:22,708 --> 00:02:25,290
Here's what's happening behind the scenes.

49
00:02:25,290 --> 00:02:29,533
A PDF stores every piece of text with its exact position on the page.

50
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:35,865
Pdfplumber finds those pieces, groups the ones that sit on the same line, and reads them top to bottom.

51
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:38,386
Each box you see here is a line it found.

52
00:02:38,386 --> 00:02:41,768
That's why the output keeps the same order as the page.

53
00:02:42,468 --> 00:02:42,970
Step two.

54
00:02:42,970 --> 00:02:45,872
Usually you don't want all the text, just one value.

55
00:02:45,872 --> 00:02:46,710
Like the total.

56
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:49,891
Text dot split lines turns the text into a list of lines.

57
00:02:49,891 --> 00:02:50,728
Loop over them.

58
00:02:50,728 --> 00:02:53,910
If a line starts with Total colon, split it at the colon.

59
00:02:53,910 --> 00:02:56,868
That gives two parts, the word Total, and the number.

60
00:02:56,868 --> 00:03:00,887
Take part one, the number, and strip removes the extra spaces around it.

61
00:03:01,587 --> 00:03:02,006
Run it.

62
00:03:02,006 --> 00:03:05,478
Found the total, three hundred fifty one point eight zero.

63
00:03:05,478 --> 00:03:07,932
That's exactly the number on the invoice.

64
00:03:07,932 --> 00:03:09,967
The same idea works for any label.

65
00:03:09,967 --> 00:03:11,823
Invoice number, date, customer.

66
00:03:11,823 --> 00:03:15,953
If the PDF writes a label and then a value, you can grab it this way.

67
00:03:16,653 --> 00:03:18,806
What about a PDF with many pages?

68
00:03:18,806 --> 00:03:20,373
Loop over pdf dot pages.

69
00:03:20,373 --> 00:03:27,094
Enumerate, with start equals one, gives you a page number that starts at one, like a human would count.

70
00:03:27,094 --> 00:03:30,358
Print a small header for each page, then its text.

71
00:03:31,058 --> 00:03:32,536
Here's the sales report.

72
00:03:32,536 --> 00:03:36,293
Page one has an introduction, and a table of sales by region.

73
00:03:36,293 --> 00:03:38,510
Page two has a list of top products.

74
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:40,358
All the text, from every page.

75
00:03:40,358 --> 00:03:41,775
But look at the tables.

76
00:03:41,775 --> 00:03:45,101
In plain text, they're just words separated by spaces.

77
00:03:45,101 --> 00:03:48,304
To get real rows and columns, we need the next step.

78
00:03:49,004 --> 00:03:50,145
Step three, tables.

79
00:03:50,145 --> 00:03:52,489
This is where pdfplumber really shines.

80
00:03:52,489 --> 00:03:55,854
Instead of extract text, call extract table on the page.

81
00:03:55,854 --> 00:04:02,223
It looks for the lines that draw the table, the grid, and uses them to split the text into rows and cells.

82
00:04:02,223 --> 00:04:03,906
You get back a list of rows.

83
00:04:03,906 --> 00:04:05,588
Each row is a list of cells.

84
00:04:06,288 --> 00:04:08,386
Watch it work on the real page.

85
00:04:08,386 --> 00:04:12,378
Pdfplumber finds the table, and reads it one row at a time.

86
00:04:12,378 --> 00:04:14,475
Region, units, revenue, growth.

87
00:04:14,475 --> 00:04:16,979
North, South, East, West, and Online.

88
00:04:16,979 --> 00:04:20,633
Every cell in the right column, exactly like the page.

89
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:23,113
And here's the printed result.

90
00:04:23,113 --> 00:04:23,647
Six rows.

91
00:04:23,647 --> 00:04:26,376
The first row is the header, the column names.

92
00:04:26,376 --> 00:04:28,630
Notice the square brackets and quotes.

93
00:04:28,630 --> 00:04:32,605
Each row is a Python list, and each cell is text, even the numbers.

94
00:04:32,605 --> 00:04:35,038
Keep that in mind, we'll come back to it.

95
00:04:35,738 --> 00:04:36,347
Step four.

96
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:37,689
Let's put it in Excel.

97
00:04:37,689 --> 00:04:38,970
Import pandas as p d.

98
00:04:38,970 --> 00:04:42,446
Then build a data frame, that's pandas' word for a table.

99
00:04:42,446 --> 00:04:46,776
The data is table, from one onward, that's every row except the header.

100
00:04:46,776 --> 00:04:49,642
And columns equals table, zero, the header row.

101
00:04:49,642 --> 00:04:54,521
Then to excel saves an Excel file, and index equals false skips the row numbers.

102
00:04:54,521 --> 00:04:56,717
To csv does the same for a CSV file.

103
00:04:57,417 --> 00:04:57,898
Run it.

104
00:04:57,898 --> 00:05:00,853
There's the table, now with proper columns.

105
00:05:00,853 --> 00:05:05,733
And two new files in the folder, sales dot x l s x and sales dot c s v.

106
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:09,719
Open the Excel file, and every value sits in its own cell.

107
00:05:09,719 --> 00:05:12,743
No copy and paste, no columns falling apart.

108
00:05:13,443 --> 00:05:14,675
Now the real shortcut.

109
00:05:14,675 --> 00:05:17,477
A folder of invoices, and one summary spreadsheet.

110
00:05:17,477 --> 00:05:21,511
Path, invoices, dot glob, star dot p d f, finds every PDF in the folder.

111
00:05:21,511 --> 00:05:24,088
For each one, read the text of the first page.

112
00:05:24,088 --> 00:05:26,441
Then check every line against four labels.

113
00:05:26,441 --> 00:05:28,739
Invoice number, date, customer and total.

114
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:31,428
When a line starts with a label, keep the value.

115
00:05:31,428 --> 00:05:34,397
Split with a one means split only at the first colon.

116
00:05:34,397 --> 00:05:36,022
Each invoice becomes one row.

117
00:05:36,722 --> 00:05:39,340
Then pandas turns all the rows into one table.

118
00:05:39,340 --> 00:05:43,836
As type float turns the totals from text into real numbers, so we can add them.

119
00:05:43,836 --> 00:05:45,715
Remember, cells come out as text.

120
00:05:45,715 --> 00:05:47,422
And sum gives the grand total.

121
00:05:47,422 --> 00:05:47,820
Run it.

122
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:52,544
Five invoices, one clean table, and a grand total of nine hundred four point eight.

123
00:05:52,544 --> 00:05:53,853
Saved as an Excel file.

124
00:05:53,853 --> 00:05:56,699
With five hundred invoices, it's the same command.

125
00:05:57,399 --> 00:05:59,260
That's the moment it pays off.

126
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:05,649
The boring job, opening each PDF, finding the number, typing it into a spreadsheet, is now one command.

127
00:06:05,649 --> 00:06:10,549
Run it again next month, on next month's folder, and the summary builds itself.

128
00:06:11,249 --> 00:06:13,154
Now, the PDFs that fight back.

129
00:06:13,154 --> 00:06:13,916
Problem one.

130
00:06:13,916 --> 00:06:19,249
Page two of the report has a table with no lines, just columns lined up with spaces.

131
00:06:19,249 --> 00:06:21,535
Run extract table, and you get None.

132
00:06:21,535 --> 00:06:22,043
Nothing.

133
00:06:22,043 --> 00:06:26,298
By default, pdfplumber looks for drawn lines, and there aren't any.

134
00:06:26,998 --> 00:06:28,414
The fix is one setting.

135
00:06:28,414 --> 00:06:31,433
Tell it to use the text itself, instead of lines.

136
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:34,821
Vertical strategy, text, and horizontal strategy, text.

137
00:06:34,821 --> 00:06:37,778
Now it splits columns at the gaps between words.

138
00:06:37,778 --> 00:06:44,862
It also returns some empty rows from the spacing, so this one line keeps only the rows that have something in them.

139
00:06:45,562 --> 00:06:46,567
And there it is.

140
00:06:46,567 --> 00:06:50,776
Product, units, revenue, and four products, each in its own column.

141
00:06:50,776 --> 00:06:54,105
The title, Top Products, came along as the first row.

142
00:06:54,105 --> 00:06:57,560
Just skip it, with table, from one onward, like before.

143
00:06:58,260 --> 00:06:59,757
Problem two, scanned PDFs.

144
00:06:59,757 --> 00:07:02,174
This receipt was photographed, or scanned.

145
00:07:02,174 --> 00:07:04,879
Run extract text, and you get two empty quotes.

146
00:07:04,879 --> 00:07:05,800
An empty string.

147
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,139
Because there is no text inside, just one picture of text.

148
00:07:09,139 --> 00:07:12,247
Pdfplumber confirms it, there's one image on the page.

149
00:07:12,247 --> 00:07:19,154
To read a scan, you need O C R, optical character recognition, which is a different tool, and a topic for its own video.

150
00:07:19,854 --> 00:07:22,049
Two more errors you'll probably meet.

151
00:07:22,049 --> 00:07:22,643
Error one.

152
00:07:22,643 --> 00:07:25,312
Module not found, no module named pdfplumber.

153
00:07:25,312 --> 00:07:28,753
The library isn't installed for the Python you're running.

154
00:07:28,753 --> 00:07:30,711
Run pip install pdfplumber again.

155
00:07:30,711 --> 00:07:37,890
If you have several Pythons, use python three dash m pip install, so it goes to the same Python you run your script with.

156
00:07:38,590 --> 00:07:39,253
Error two.

157
00:07:39,253 --> 00:07:40,249
File not found.

158
00:07:40,249 --> 00:07:41,909
Look closely at the name.

159
00:07:41,909 --> 00:07:47,552
I typed invoice dash ten forty two, but the file is invoice underscore ten forty two.

160
00:07:47,552 --> 00:07:49,410
The name must match exactly.

161
00:07:49,410 --> 00:07:53,062
And the terminal must be in the same folder as the PDF.

162
00:07:53,062 --> 00:07:54,854
Use c d to get there first.

163
00:07:55,554 --> 00:07:56,024
Pro tip.

164
00:07:56,024 --> 00:07:57,670
Keep these five lines handy.

165
00:07:57,670 --> 00:07:59,434
Extract text for all the text.

166
00:07:59,434 --> 00:08:04,606
Extract table for one table, and extract tables, with an s, for every table on the page.

167
00:08:04,606 --> 00:08:08,604
Crop, with a box, reads only part of the page, like just the header.

168
00:08:08,604 --> 00:08:13,718
And extract words gives you every word with its exact position, for the tricky layouts.

169
00:08:14,418 --> 00:08:15,195
Quick recap.

170
00:08:15,195 --> 00:08:16,427
Install pdfplumber.

171
00:08:16,427 --> 00:08:18,954
Open the file with pdfplumber dot open.

172
00:08:18,954 --> 00:08:22,778
Extract text for the text, and split lines to find a value.

173
00:08:22,778 --> 00:08:26,601
Extract table for tables, and pandas to save them to Excel.

174
00:08:26,601 --> 00:08:28,934
Loop over a folder for the big jobs.

175
00:08:28,934 --> 00:08:32,240
And if a table has no lines, use the text strategy.

176
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:35,625
Next on Shortcut Lab, regex for beginners.

177
00:08:35,625 --> 00:08:43,809
One small pattern that finds every email, phone number or date in any text, including the text you just pulled out of your PDFs.

178
00:08:43,809 --> 00:08:45,855
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
